 {
  "source": "Deutsche Bank",
  "title": "Can markets pull off goldilocks, or does something have to give?",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "high",
  "published": "2026-08-17",
  "insights": [
   "Current market pricing reflects a 'goldilocks' scenario of resilient growth, limited central bank rate hikes, and contained supply shocks, which the bank views as an unsustainable equilibrium.",
   "Bloomberg's index of US financial conditions reached its most accommodative level since 1996, suggesting markets are pricing an immaculate outcome that leaves no margin for error.",
   "Scenario 1: If growth remains robust, financial conditions will stay accommodative, forcing central banks to hike rates more aggressively than currently priced, potentially catching markets off guard.",
   "Scenario 2: If growth slows, the current underpinning for risk assets will fade, likely leading to a correction even in the absence of an outright recession.",
   "Historical precedents like 2015-16 and 2022 demonstrate that growth slowdowns without formal recessions can still trigger significant bear markets for equities.",
   "The oil futures curve is currently downward-sloping despite the Strait of Hormuz remaining blocked, indicating that markets are underpricing potential supply shocks.",
   "The S&P 500 is on track for a 4th consecutive year of double-digit gains, a feat not seen since the 1990s, further highlighting the extreme optimism currently priced in."
  ],
